Let's consider a scenario where an application generates sales reports using a database.

Initially, the code is tightly coupled to a specific database type SQL Server.

```text
// SalesReport class with tight coupling to SQL Server
public class SalesReport
{
    private readonly SqlServerDatabase _database;

    public SalesReport()
    {
        _database = new SqlServerDatabase();
    }

    // Report generation and other methods
    // ...
}

// SQL Server specific database implementation
public class SqlServerDatabase
{
    // SQL Server specific implementation
    // ...
}
```

However, we want to make the application adaptable to support different database types, such as Cassandra.

```text
// IDatabase interface representing common database operations
public interface IDatabase
{
    void Connect();
    void Insert();
    void Update();
    void Delete();
    void Select();
}

// Implementation for SQL Server database
public class SqlServerDatabase : IDatabase
{
    // SQL Server specific implementation
    // ...
}

// Implementation for Cassandra database
public class CassandraDatabase : IDatabase
{
    // Cassandra specific implementation
    // ...
}

// SalesReport class using IDatabase interface
public class SalesReport
{
    private readonly IDatabase _database;

    public SalesReport(IDatabase database)
    {
        _database = database;
    }

    // Report generation and other methods
    // ...
}
```

In the above code, we introduce the IDatabase interface, which defines the common database operations.

Both the SqlServerDatabase and CassandraDatabase classes implement this interface, representing different database types.

The SalesReport class no longer depends on the concrete database implementations but instead relies on the abstraction provided by the IDatabase interface.

