The Decorator pattern allows us to add caching without modifying existing code.

We use the Decorator pattern to wrap the existing WeatherService instance with caching functionality.

```text
public class CachedWeatherService(WeatherService weatherService, IMemoryCache memoryCache) : IWeatherService
{ 
  public async Task<WeatherResult> GetWeatherForecast()
  {
      var cacheKey = $"{nameof(CachedWeatherService)}-{nameof(GetWeatherForecast)}";

      return memoryCache.GetOrCreate(cacheKey, entry =>
      {
          entry.AbsoluteExpiration = DateTime.Now.AddHours(1);
          entry.Priority = CacheItemPriority.High;
          entry.SlidingExpiration = TimeSpan.FromMinutes(30);
          return weatherService.GetWeatherForecast();
      });
  }
}
```

In the above code, we are creating a new class CachedWeatherService which implements the IWeatherService interface.

