GroupBy() is a very popular thing, a very common thing that you would do in many applications where organizing, summarizing, and analyzing data in a more meaningful way.

We can group elements in sequences (arrays, lists, etc.) based on a key value.

The result is a new sequence that contains elements with the matching key element and their associated collection values.

```text
List<Product> products = GetProducts();

//Method Syntax
List<IGrouping<string, Product>> result = products
                                           .GroupBy(product => product.Color)
                                           .ToList();

//Query Syntax
List<IGrouping<string, Product>> result = (from product in products
                                           group product by product.Color)
                                           .ToList();
```

GroupJoin() is a very popular thing, a very common thing that you would do in many applications where you have a one‑to‑many relationship.

We can combine elements between two or more sequences (arrays, lists, etc.) based on a key value.

The result is a new sequence that contains elements with the matching key element and their associated collection values.

For Example, new collection of customerOrders for each customer.

```text
List<Product> products = GetProducts();
List<Sale> sales = GetSales();
List<ProductSales> result = new();

//Method Syntax
List<ProductSales> result = products
                           .GroupJoin(sales, 
                                 product => product.Id,
                                 sale => sale.ProductId,
                                 (product, productSales) => new ProductSales
                                 {
                                    Product = product,
                                    Sales = productSales.ToList()
                                 })
                           .ToList();

//Query Syntax
List<ProductSales> result = (from product in products
                            join sale in sales 
                            on product.Id equals sale.ProductId
                            into productSales
                            select new ProductSales
                             {
                                Product = product,
                                Sales = productSales.ToList()
                             }) 
                           .ToList();
```

